  "script": {
    "body": "Almost every country in Europe overdubbed foreign shows. The Netherlands kept 90% subtitled. It started as a money problem, not a culture one. Dubbing one film cost 10x what subtitles cost. The market was small. The budgets were smaller. Three things followed from one accounting decision. Kids grew up reading their television. Today 90% of Dutch adults speak English. Cheapness became fluency. Nobody planned it. But the interesting part is what it did to comedy. Timing survives translation. Dubbed jokes rarely do. Dutch TV exported 4x more than it imported. A budget cut turned into a national advantage. Constraints do that more often than money does. Subtitles were never the compromise.",
    "fullText": "The Dutch refused to dub their TV. Almost every country in Europe overdubbed foreign shows. The Netherlands kept 90% subtitled. It started as a money problem, not a culture one. Dubbing one film cost 10x what subtitles cost. The market was small. The budgets were smaller. Three things followed from one accounting decision. Kids grew up reading their television. Today 90% of Dutch adults speak English. Cheapness became fluency. Nobody planned it. But the interesting part is what it did to comedy. Timing survives translation. Dubbed jokes rarely do. Dutch TV exported 4x more than it imported. A budget cut turned into a national advantage. Constraints do that more often than money does. Subtitles were never the compromise.",
    "hook": "The Dutch refused to dub their TV.",
    "pronunciation": [
      {
        "display": "90%",
        "rule": "percent",
        "spoken": "ninety percent"
      },
      {
        "display": "90%",
        "rule": "percent",
        "spoken": "ninety percent"
      },
      {
        "display": "10x",
        "rule": "multiplier",
        "spoken": "ten times"
      },
      {
        "display": "4x",
        "rule": "multiplier",
        "spoken": "four times"
      },
      {
        "display": "TV",
        "rule": "acronym",
        "spoken": "T V"
      },
      {
        "display": "TV",
        "rule": "acronym",
        "spoken": "T V"
      }
    ],
    "spokenText": "The Dutch refused to dub their T V. Almost every country in Europe overdubbed foreign shows. The Netherlands kept ninety percent subtitled. It started as a money problem, not a culture one. Dubbing one film cost ten times what subtitles cost. The market was small. The budgets were smaller. Three things followed from one accounting decision. Kids grew up reading their television. Today ninety percent of Dutch adults speak English. Cheapness became fluency. Nobody planned it. But the interesting part is what it did to comedy. Timing survives translation. Dubbed jokes rarely do. Dutch T V exported four times more than it imported. A budget cut turned into a national advantage. Constraints do that more often than money does. Subtitles were never the compromise."
  },
